}
```

The reason why I want this so I can remove some boiler plate from my component system written using R6 inside handy function that make code shorter.

I've tried multiple things `eval substitute` changing environment initilizing with NULL and overwriting new function and they all didn't work, is there a way to make function that return R6Class work?

Hi,

Here is a short reprex to illustrate my problem:

```
library(R6)

Family <- R6Class(
  "Family",
  private = list(
     shared = {
       env <- new.env()
       env$address <- "1547 Coffman Alley"
       env
     }
   ),
   active = list(
     address = function(value){
       if(missing(value)) return(private$shared$address)
       else private$shared$address <- value
     }
   )
  )

Child <- R6Class(
  "ChildShared", 
   inherit = Family,
  private = list(
    shared = {
      env <- new.env()
      env$school <- "River Bridge School"
      env
    }
  ),
  active = list(
    school = function(value){
      if(missing(value)) return(private$shared$school)
      else private$shared$school <- value
    }
  )
)


mother <- Family$new()
mother$address
child_1 = Child$new()
child_1$address # NULL => why isn't it "1547 Coffman Alley"
child_1$school # ok : "River Bridge School"

```

Why does `child_1$address` is  NULL?

I would expect that my`Child` class would inherits from the `Family` class shared env even if it has is proper shared env. How to allow a child class to have its proper shared env while still inheriting from its parent class shared env? 

Is there a trick or is it a R6 missing feature? 

Thanks for your help.

I'd like to find a way to constrain fields, that is, passing tests in a similar way to the Moose Object-oriented system in Perl. This is a suggested enhancement or extension in another package to R6. 

What I want to achieve can be done using active fields, though the coding is kind of convoluted and the field value isn't shown in the R6.print method. 
An example of this is:
```{r}
library(R6)
library(checkmate)

CONSTRAINT <- R6Class(
  "CONSTRAINT",
  public = list(

  ),
  private = list(
    x_ = 0
  ),
  active = list(
    x = function(value) {
      if (missing(value)) {
        return(private$x_)
      } else {
        check <- checkmate::checkNumber(value)
        if(is.character(check)) {
          stop("Value attempting to be assigned to x is not valid: ", check)
        }
        value
      }
    }
  )
)

A <- CONSTRAINT$new()
A$x <- 5 # Successful
A$x <- 1L # Successful
A$x <- pi # Successful
A$x <- c(3,5) # Throws an error, as required. 
A$x <- "SD" # Throws an error, as required. 
```

What I would be hoping for is something like this:

```{r}
CONSTRAINT_IDEAL <- R6Class(
  "CONSTRAINT",
  public = list(
    x = 0
  ),
  constraints = list(
    x = checkmate::checkNumber # this can be any function that returns TRUE when the field is within the constraints.
  )
)
```

My main problem is I don't know if this is a possible extension of public/private fields or whether this would have to stick to active bindings (with a rewrite of the R6.print method to show the values of these constrained fields).

I'm willing to have an attempt at developing this myself, though I need to understand much better how R6 works under the hood.

